  "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $N?$\n\nI. $N∨(¬M∨N)$\n\nII. $N∧(¬M∨N)$\n\nIII. $(¬M∨N)∧N$",
  "worked_solution": "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nN∧(N∨¬M)≡N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $N.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $N.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N∧(¬M∨N) & ≡N∧(N∨¬M) \\\\  & ≡N.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $N.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬M∨N)∧N & ≡N∧(¬M∨N) \\\\  & ≡N∧(N∨¬M) \\\\  & ≡N.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
